      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Wilder officially became a home‑rule city on March 15, 1935, solidifying its status within Campbell County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/wilder_kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first settlement, Leitch's Station, appeared around 1789–1791 when Major David Leitch recruited about 20 settlers near the Licking River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/wilder_kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the Civil War, Wilder hosted the Holt Battery, a Union fortification built by General Joseph Holt, and the nearby Wiggins Battery.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/wilder_kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Early residents were largely employees of Andrews Steel, later Newport Steel, anchoring Wilder’s industrial identity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/wilder_kentucky",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Wilder’s location near the Ohio and Licking Rivers makes it prone to spring floods, a pattern seen in the 1937 disaster.",
